.docname {Telemetry}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Reading | One value from one room at one moment | 24.5 °C in room `r2` |
    | Batch | One building's readings sent in one request | 40 readings, `POST /telemetry/ingest` |
    | Metric | A kind of reading, handled by one plugin | `temperature`, `airQuality` |
    | Plugin | One file that declares a metric: fields, unit, limits, commands | `plugins/temperature.rs` |
    | Device kind | What a sensor physically is. One device can report several metrics | `router` reports 3 counts |
    | Bounds | The limits of one metric, as named numbers | `{"maxTemp": 28, "minTemp": 16}` |
    | Breach | A field past its limit | 30 °C with `maxTemp` 28 |
    | HMAC | A signature only the holder of a key can make | `x-signature` on every batch |
    | Device key | The key one building's devices sign with, derived from a master key | `hex(HMAC(master, "b1:3"))` |
    | Epoch | A counter per building. Bumping it changes the building's key | 3 |
    | Microkernel | A small core that knows nothing about the specific kinds of data. Each kind plugs in from outside | `kernel/` and `plugins/` |
    | Tick | Everything one building reports at one moment, as one message | one Wi-Fi snapshot |
    | Hypertable | A PostgreSQL table that TimescaleDB splits by time, so recent data is fast and old data compresses | `readings` |
    | Port | What the kernel needs, in its own words | `ReadingStore` |
    | Adapter | The code that does it with a real technology | `PgReadings` |

`telemetry` is a Rust service (Axum, PostgreSQL with TimescaleDB, Kafka, Redis) in `backend/telemetry`. For the role and the choices behind it, read [Telemetry architecture](../architecture/telemetry-architecture.qd). For storage, [Telemetry Storage](../design/telemetry-storage.qd).

It is **the door for every sensor reading**. It checks, stores and hands on readings. It also owns the limits, the sensors and the device commands, and it raises an alert for every breach.

---

## The idea in one minute

Imagine the mail room of a big building.

- **Sensors drop off parcels** (readings). A parcel must have the right label: which building, which room, which kind of sensor, and a value. A parcel with a wrong label is refused, and so is the *whole batch* it came in.
- At the door, a **guard checks the seal** (the signature). Only a device that knows the building's key can make a valid seal.
- Accepted parcels go in three places at once: into the **archive** (PostgreSQL), onto the **live belt** for the dashboards (Redis), and, if a value is past its limit, a **red slip** goes to the alarm office (the `alerts` topic on Kafka).
- Each kind of sensor is a **plugin**: one small file that says "I am temperature, my value is in this field, my limits are `maxTemp` and `minTemp`". The mail room itself does not know what a temperature is. Adding a new kind of sensor means adding one file.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        S["sensors, simulators"] -- "POST /ingest (signed)" --> T[("telemetry<br/>the mail room")]
        C["ap-collector"] -- "POST /observations" --> T
        T --> PG[("PostgreSQL<br/>the archive")]
        T -- "telemetry:raw" --> R["Redis, then dashboard"]
        T -- "alerts" --> K["Kafka, then notification"]
        T -- "wifi-observations" --> W["digital-twin"]
        W -- "estimated-occupancy" --> T
        Tw["digital-twin"] <-- "registration" --> T

---

## The files

Ports and adapters around a **microkernel**. `tests/architecture.rs` fails the build when a rule breaks.

| Path | What it holds | Must not import |
|---|---|---|
| `src/types/` | Plain shapes: `Reading`, `Sensor`, `Bounds`, `BoundSpec`, `SensorPlugin`, `DomainError` | anything else in the crate, any IO crate |
| `src/kernel/` | The use cases: `ingest`, `observations`, `occupancy`, `readings`, `thresholds`, `sensors`, `actions`, `registration`, `simulation`, `devices`, `authz`, `registry`, plus `ports.rs` | `plugins`, `adapters`, IO crates |
| `src/plugins/` | `temperature`, `air_quality`, `people_count`, `device_count`, `common` | `kernel`, `adapters`, IO crates, **another plugin** |
| `src/adapters/driven/` | `postgres/`, `redis_fanout`, `kafka_producer`, `twin_directory`, `dispatch`, `simulators`, `threshold_cache`, `building_cache` | |
| `src/adapters/driving/` | `http_api/` (`controllers`, `claims`, `exceptions`), `kafka_consumer`, `occupancy_consumer` | `driven` |
| `src/adapters/ingest_auth.rs` | Signature checks, device keys | |
| `src/lib.rs`, `src/main.rs` | The router. Start-up wiring | |
| `migrations/` | `0001` to `0007`, run at start | |

IO crates the kernel may never name: `sqlx`, `redis`, `rdkafka`, `axum`, `reqwest`, `prometheus`. The kernel is pure logic, so its tests run against in-memory fakes.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        H["adapters/driving<br/>HTTP, Kafka consumers"] --> K["kernel<br/>Ingest, Observations, Registration, ..."]
        K --> T["types<br/>Reading, Bounds, SensorPlugin"]
        K --> P{{"ports"}}
        P -.-> A["adapters/driven<br/>Postgres, Redis, Kafka"]
        M["main.rs"] --> PL["plugins<br/>temperature, airQuality, ..."]
        PL --> T
        M --> K

The kernel and the plugins **never import each other**. Only `main.rs` brings them together.

---

## Plugins: one file per kind of sensor

A plugin has to answer five questions. They are the `SensorPlugin` trait in `types/plugin.rs`:

```rust
pub trait SensorPlugin: Send + Sync {
    fn key(&self) -> &'static str;
    fn descriptor(&self) -> &MetricDescriptor;
    fn validate(&self, payload: &Value) -> Result<Reading, Vec<String>>;
    fn bounds(&self) -> &'static [BoundSpec];

    fn actions(&self) -> &'static [ActionSpec] {
        &[]
    }
}
```

- `key`: its name (`temperature`). This is the `type` a reading carries.
- `descriptor`: label, unit, and the list of fields a reading must have.
- `validate`: turn raw JSON into a checked `Reading`, or a list of complaints.
- `bounds`: which limits it has.
- `actions`: which commands a device can get (default: none).

### The field specs

```rust
pub enum FieldKind {
    NonEmptyString,
    Finite,
    NonNegativeInt,
}

pub struct FieldSpec {
    pub name: &'static str,
    pub kind: FieldKind,
    pub required: bool,
}

fn accepts(kind: FieldKind, value: &Value) -> bool {
    match kind {
        FieldKind::NonEmptyString => value.as_str().is_some_and(|s| !s.is_empty()),
        FieldKind::Finite => value.as_f64().is_some(),
        FieldKind::NonNegativeInt => value.as_f64().is_some_and(|n| n >= 0.0 && n.fract() == 0.0),
    }
}
```

A field is a name, a kind and "required or not". `check_fields` runs every spec and collects messages like `temperature: must be a finite number.` A missing optional field (or `null`) is fine.

### A whole plugin: temperature

```rust
static DESCRIPTOR: MetricDescriptor = MetricDescriptor {
    value_field: "temperature",
    key: "temperature",
    label: "Temperature",
    interface_name: "ITemperature",
    unit: Some("C"),
    fields: &[
        FieldSpec { name: "buildingId", kind: FieldKind::NonEmptyString, required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "roomId",     kind: FieldKind::NonEmptyString, required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "timestamp",  kind: FieldKind::Finite,         required: true },
        FieldSpec { name: "temperature",kind: FieldKind::Finite,         required: true },
    ],
};
```

What a temperature reading must contain. `value_field` says which field holds *the* value (the one stored in the `value` column).

```rust
static BOUNDS: &[BoundSpec] = &[
    BoundSpec { key: "maxTemp", field: "temperature", label: "Temperature", unit: Some("°C"), direction: BoundDirection::Above },
    BoundSpec { key: "minTemp", field: "temperature", label: "Temperature", unit: Some("°C"), direction: BoundDirection::Below },
];
```

Two limits. `key` is the name of the limit in the stored bounds (`maxTemp`). `field` is the payload field it is compared with. `direction` says "breach when above" or "breach when below".

```rust
fn validate(&self, payload: &Value) -> Result<Reading, Vec<String>> {
    let errors = check_fields(DESCRIPTOR.fields, payload);
    if !errors.is_empty() {
        return Err(errors);
    }
    let value = payload[DESCRIPTOR.value_field].as_f64().unwrap_or_default();
    Ok(reading(payload, DESCRIPTOR.key, value))
}
```

`validate` is short: check the fields, and if there are no complaints, read the value and build the `Reading`. The actions are `setTarget(target)`, `increase(step?)` and `decrease(step?)`.

### What each plugin declares

| Plugin key | Value field | Bounds (key, field, direction) | Actions | Device kind |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `temperature` | `temperature` | `maxTemp` high, `minTemp` low, both on `temperature` | `setTarget(target)`, `increase(step?)`, `decrease(step?)` | `temperature` (Thermostat) |
| `airQuality` | `indoor_aqi` | `maxCo2` high on `co2`, `maxAqi` high on `indoor_aqi` | none | `airQuality` |
| `peopleCount` | `peopleCount` | `maxPeople` high | none | `peopleCount` |
| `totalDeviceCount` | `totalDeviceCount` | none | none | `router` |
| `ratioDeviceCount` | `ratioDeviceCount` | none | none | `router` |
| `estimatedOccupancy` | `estimatedOccupancy` | none | none | `router` |

- `PluginRegistry::new` refuses two plugins with one key.
- `DeviceCatalog::new` refuses a metric with no device, or with two. The service does not start.
- A plugin with bounds must be in `telemetry_schema::ALERTABLE_METRICS`, or a test fails. See [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd). That list is also how `notification` knows which metrics to deliver.
- A **sensor is a device, not a metric**. A `router` is one device that reports three metrics. A sensor's actions are the union of its device's metrics' actions.

**Adding a metric** = a plugin file, plus its line in `plugins::all()`, plus a device in `plugins::devices()`.

---

## Start-up

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant M as main.rs
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        participant K as Kafka
        participant R as Redis
        M->>M: probe_exit_code: started with --health?
        M->>M: read DATABASE_URL and TELEMETRY_DEVICE_MASTER_KEY, both required
        M->>PG: pool of 10, then sqlx::migrate!
        M->>M: PluginRegistry::new(plugins::all())
        M->>M: DeviceCatalog::new(plugins::devices())
        M->>M: DeviceKeys::new(master, shared key)
        M->>M: HttpDispatch::from_json(bindings.json)
        M->>R: RedisFanout::connect
        M->>M: HttpSimulators::from_json(SIMULATORS), Simulation::new
        M->>K: KafkaEvents::connect, ensure_topics in the background
        M->>M: build Ingest, Registration, AppState
        M->>K: spawn kafka_consumer, group building-registrations
        M->>K: spawn occupancy_consumer, group estimated-occupancy
        M->>M: bind 0.0.0.0:PORT, serve telemetry::router

Any `?` in this sequence stops the process: a missing key, a short key (under 32 bytes), a failed migration, a plugin clash, a bad `SIMULATORS`. Kafka being down does **not** stop it: topics are created in the background, and produces fail after 5 s until it is back.

---

## Routes

Every field and status code: [Telemetry API](../api/index.html?spec=telemetry.yaml) (OpenAPI).

The edge strips `/telemetry`. Three **exact** paths are open at the edge and checked here by signature: `/telemetry/ingest`, `/telemetry/collector`, `/telemetry/observations`. Any sub-path, like `/telemetry/ingest/batch`, falls into the gated block and gets `401`. The same three are listed in the Istio no-principal rule. Every other route needs the gateway pass.

| Method | Path | Who calls | Check | What it does |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/ingest` | Devices, simulators | Body HMAC | Store a batch |
| `POST` | `/observations` | `ap-collector` | Body HMAC | Count a Wi-Fi tick, pass it to Kafka |
| `GET` | `/collector?buildingId=` | `ap-collector` | Timestamp HMAC | Routers to poll |
| `GET` | `/contracts` | `dashboard` | none | Metric catalog |
| `GET` | `/devices` | `client` | none | Device kinds |
| `GET` | `/health`, `/metrics` | Probes, Prometheus | none | |
| `GET` | `/{metric}/latest?building=&roomId=` | `client` | read | Newest reading of one room |
| `GET` | `/{metric}/entireBuilding?building=` | `client` | read | Newest reading of each room |
| `GET` | `/{metric}/dashboard?building=&roomId=&timeRange=&start=&end=&aggMode=` | `client` | read | Buckets over time |
| `GET` | `/connected-devices/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read | Building device total |
| `GET` | `/thresholds/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read | Temperature limits, building and rooms |
| `PUT` | `/thresholds/buildings/{id}` | `digital-twin` | edit | Register name, rooms, optional `maxTemperature` |
| `GET`, `PATCH` | `/thresholds/{metric}/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read, edit | Building bounds |
| `PATCH` | `/thresholds/{metric}/buildings/{id}/rooms` | `client` | edit | Bounds of many rooms, all or nothing |
| `PATCH` | `/thresholds/{metric}/buildings/{id}/rooms/{roomId}` | `client` | edit | Bounds of one room |
| `GET`, `POST` | `/sensors/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read, edit | List. Apply a batch |
| `GET` | `/sensors/buildings/{id}/rooms/{roomId}` | `client` | read | Sensors of one room |
| `POST` | `/device-keys/buildings/{id}` | `client` | edit | New device key |
| `GET`, `PUT`, `DELETE` | `/simulation/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read, edit, edit | Status, start, stop |
| `POST` | `/executeAction` | `client` | edit | Send a command to a device |

### Access check

`read` and `edit` in `controllers.rs` run before any data is touched:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as controllers.rs
        participant D as TwinDirectory
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant A as kernel/authz.rs
        C->>C: GatewayClaims: header present, decodes, has sub
        C->>D: domains_of(building, raw claims)
        D->>D: cache younger than 60 s?
        D->>Tw: GET /domain/{building}, x-gateway-claims, 5 s timeout
        Tw-->>D: ["eng"]
        D->>D: cache 60 s
        C->>A: read: is_member_of(claims, "eng")<br/>edit: can_edit_domains(claims, ["eng"])
        A-->>C: allow, or 403 Not authorized for this building

`telemetry` does not know who owns a building. It asks `digital-twin`, forwarding the caller's own claims, then lets Cedar decide.

| Case | Answer |
|---|---|
| No header, bad base64, no `sub` | `401` |
| `digital-twin` down, slow, or non-2xx | `403`. A failed lookup is never cached |
| Caller in no owning domain | `403`, `telemetry_authz_denials_total{action}` + 1 |

`edit` uses Cedar with the shared bundle from `backend/libs/auth-policy`. A role Cedar does not know is ignored, never treated as "allow all".

### Error body

Every error is `{"type": "...", "message": "..."}`:

| `DomainError` | Status | `type` |
|---|---|---|
| `Validation` | `400` | `Validation Error` |
| `Rejected` | `422` | `Rejected Error`, plus `errors: [{ref, field, message}]` |
| `NotFound` | `404` | `Not Found Error` |
| `Forbidden` | `403` | `Forbidden Error` |
| `BadGateway` | `502` | `Bad Gateway Error` |
| `Internal` | `500` | `Internal Server Error`. The real error only in the log |

---

## The seal on a batch: `ingest_auth.rs`

Devices do not have a person's JWT. They sign each request instead.

### A key, and how to sign

```rust
pub fn sign(&self, body: &[u8]) -> String {
    let mut mac = Hmac::<Sha256>::new_from_slice(&self.0).expect("hmac accepts keys of any length");
    mac.update(body);
    mac.finalize().into_bytes().iter().fold(String::new(), |mut hex, byte| {
        let _ = write!(hex, "{byte:02x}");
        hex
    })
}
```

HMAC-SHA256 over the body bytes, written as lowercase hex. A key shorter than 32 bytes is refused when it is made (`IngestKey::new`), so a weak secret fails at start.

### One key per building, never stored

```rust
/// The building's key at `epoch`: hex HMAC(master, "{building}:{epoch}").
pub fn derive(&self, building_id: &str, epoch: i32) -> String {
    self.master.sign(format!("{building_id}:{epoch}").as_bytes())
}

pub async fn issue(&self, building_id: &str) -> anyhow::Result<Option<String>> {
    let epoch = self.epochs.rotate(building_id).await?;
    Ok(epoch.map(|epoch| self.derive(building_id, epoch)))
}
```

There is one **master key** (`TELEMETRY_DEVICE_MASTER_KEY`). A building's key is the HMAC of the master key over `"{buildingId}:{epoch}"`. So:

- No device key is ever stored. The only stored state is the building's `device_key_epoch` number.
- To **revoke** a key, bump the epoch (`issue`). The old key stops working at once, and the new key is shown once.
- A leaked key of building `b1` does not open `b2`: the epoch string is different.

**Example.** `b1` at epoch 2. Rotation sets epoch 3. A collector still signing with the epoch 2 key gets `401` until it is given the new key. `b2` is not affected.

### Who is accepted

```rust
pub async fn accepts(&self, building_id: Option<&str>, message: &[u8], signature: &str) -> bool {
    let signed_by = |key: &IngestKey| constant_time_eq(&key.sign(message), signature);
    if self.shared.as_ref().is_some_and(signed_by) {
        return true;
    }
    let Some(building_id) = building_id else { return false; };
    match self.epochs.epoch(building_id).await {
        Ok(Some(epoch)) => IngestKey::new(&self.derive(building_id, epoch)).is_ok_and(|key| signed_by(&key)),
        Ok(None) => false,
        Err(error) => { log::warn!("device key epoch unreadable for {building_id}: {error:?}"); false }
    }
}
```

1. The optional **shared key** (`TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET`) signs for any building. It is for local runs and simulators only.
2. Otherwise, work out the building's key from its **current** epoch, and compare.
3. Unknown building, or an epoch that cannot be read: not accepted.

The epoch is read from PostgreSQL on **every** request, never cached, so a rotated key stops working on every copy of the service at once.

```rust
fn constant_time_eq(a: &str, b: &str) -> bool {
    a.len() == b.len()
        && a.bytes().zip(b.bytes()).fold(0u8, |acc, (x, y)| acc | (x ^ y)) == 0
}
```

The comparison looks at **every** byte and never stops early, so the time it takes does not reveal how many leading characters of a guess were right.

### The middleware for a batch

```rust
pub async fn verify_signature(State(keys): State<DeviceKeys>, request: Request, next: Next) -> Result<Response, StatusCode> {
    let Some(signature) = request.headers().get(SIGNATURE_HEADER).and_then(|v| v.to_str().ok()).map(str::to_owned)
    else {
        metrics::record_ingest("unknown", "unsigned");
        return Err(StatusCode::UNAUTHORIZED);
    };

    let (parts, body) = request.into_parts();
    let bytes = axum::body::to_bytes(body, MAX_BODY_BYTES).await.map_err(|_| StatusCode::PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE)?;

    // Read only to pick the key; the handler still validates the whole body.
    let building = serde_json::from_slice::<Addressed>(&bytes).ok();
    let building_id = building.as_ref().map(|b| b.building_id.as_str());
    if !keys.accepts(building_id, &bytes, &signature).await {
        metrics::record_ingest("unknown", "bad_signature");
        return Err(StatusCode::UNAUTHORIZED);
    }

    Ok(next.run(Request::from_parts(parts, Body::from(bytes))).await)
}
```

1. No `x-signature`: `401`, counted `unsigned`.
2. Read the body, at most 1 MiB (`1 << 20`). More: `413`.
3. Peek at `buildingId` only to **choose the key**. The real handler still validates the whole body.
4. Wrong seal: `401`, counted `bad_signature`.
5. Put the very same bytes back and let the request through.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        S{"x-signature header?"} -- no --> U1["401, ingest unsigned"]
        S -- yes --> B{"body 1 MiB or less?"}
        B -- no --> P["413"]
        B -- yes --> Sh{"TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET set<br/>and it signs the body?"}
        Sh -- yes --> Ok["pass on"]
        Sh -- no --> Id{"body has a buildingId?"}
        Id -- no --> U2["401, bad signature"]
        Id -- yes --> Ep{"building registered,<br/>epoch readable?"}
        Ep -- no --> U2
        Ep -- yes --> K["key = hex(HMAC(master, id:epoch))"]
        K --> M{"HMAC(key, body) equals header,<br/>constant-time compare?"}
        M -- no --> U2
        M -- yes --> Ok

- The signature is over the **exact bytes** sent. A body formatted again by a proxy fails.
- The same golden vectors (`schemas/fixtures/internal-signature.json`) are checked by the Go services, so the scheme cannot drift on one side.

### The collector's GET

A `GET` has no body, so the collector signs a made-up message instead:

```rust
pub fn collector_message(building_id: Option<&str>, timestamp: &str) -> String {
    format!("GET /collector\n{}\n{timestamp}", building_id.unwrap_or_default())
}

pub fn is_fresh(timestamp: &str, now_s: i64) -> bool {
    timestamp.parse::<i64>().is_ok_and(|stamped| (now_s - stamped).abs() <= MAX_SKEW_S)   // 300
}
```

The signed text is `GET /collector`, the building, and the time (`x-timestamp`). `verify_collector_request` accepts only a signature that is valid **and** fresh (within 300 s of the server's clock). A captured request is useless after 5 minutes.

Without `buildingId` only the shared key passes, and it reads every building.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant A as ap-collector
        participant V as verify_collector_request
        participant H as controllers::collector
        A->>V: GET /telemetry/collector?buildingId=b1<br/>x-timestamp 1761300000, x-signature
        V->>V: message = collector_message(b1, timestamp)
        V->>V: is_fresh: within 300 s of now?
        V->>V: DeviceKeys::accepts(b1, message, signature)
        V->>H: pass on
        H->>H: Sensors::for_collector: routers in rooms, by building, sorted
        H-->>A: {"buildings":[{"buildingId":"b1","routers":[…]}]}

Each router in the answer has `sensorId`, `roomId`, and `driver`, `endpoint` only when set. **Never a login.**

---

## Ingesting a batch: `kernel/ingest.rs`

This is the heart of the service.

```rust
pub const MAX_BATCH_READINGS: usize = 500;

pub struct Ingest {
    pub registry: Arc<PluginRegistry>,
    pub readings: Arc<dyn ReadingStore>,
    pub thresholds: Arc<dyn ThresholdStore>,
    pub fanout: Arc<dyn Fanout>,
    pub alerts: Arc<dyn Alerts>,
    pub buildings: Arc<dyn BuildingStore>,
    pub clock: Arc<dyn Clock>,
}
```

Seven collaborators. Six are ports. `registry` is the list of plugins. The clock is a port so tests can fix the time.

### Step 1: check everything first

```rust
pub async fn accept(&self, building_id: &str, items: &[Value]) -> Result<usize, DomainError> {
    if items.len() > MAX_BATCH_READINGS {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation(format!("readings: must not exceed {MAX_BATCH_READINGS} per batch.")));
    }

    let mut errors = Vec::new();
    if building_id.trim().is_empty() {
        errors.push("buildingId: must be a non-empty string.".to_owned());
    }
    if items.is_empty() {
        errors.push("readings: must not be empty.".to_owned());
    }

    let mut readings = Vec::with_capacity(items.len());
    for (index, item) in items.iter().enumerate() {
        match self.reading_from(building_id, item) {
            Ok(reading) => readings.push(reading),
            Err(message) => errors.push(format!("readings[{index}]: {message}")),
        }
    }
    if !errors.is_empty() {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation(errors.join(" ")));
    }
```

- More than 500 readings: refused **before any work**.
- An empty batch or a blank building: complaints.
- Then every reading is checked, and **all** the complaints are collected, joined, and returned as one `400`. Nothing is stored, nothing is published. A batch is all or nothing.

### What happens to one reading: `reading_from`

```rust
let Some(object) = item.as_object() else { return Err("must be an object.".to_owned()); };

let metric = object.get("type").and_then(Value::as_str).map(str::trim).filter(|m| !m.is_empty())
    .ok_or_else(|| "type: must be a non-empty string.".to_owned())?;

let plugin = self.registry.get(metric).ok_or_else(|| format!("type: unknown sensor type: {metric}."))?;

let mut payload: Map<String, Value> = object.clone();
payload.remove("type");
if !building_id.trim().is_empty() {
    if let Some(own) = object.get("buildingId").and_then(Value::as_str) && own != building_id {
        return Err(format!("buildingId: must match the batch's building ({building_id})."));
    }
    payload.insert("buildingId".to_owned(), Value::from(building_id));
}

plugin.validate(&Value::Object(payload)).map_err(|messages| messages.join(" "))
```

1. Not an object: `must be an object.`
2. `type` missing or blank: `type: must be a non-empty string.`
3. No plugin with that key: `type: unknown sensor type: humidity.`
4. Copy the reading without `type`.
5. If the reading names a **different** building from the batch: `buildingId: must match the batch's building (b1).` Otherwise the batch's `buildingId` is stamped in. `buildingId` rides on the batch, so producers stop sending the field that could disagree.
6. Hand the payload to the **plugin**: `plugin.validate`. This is the only place the kernel meets a specific kind of sensor, and it does it through the trait.

**Example.** A batch of 40 readings for `b1`. Reading 17 has no `roomId`. Reading 30 says `"type":"humidity"`.

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| Count | 40, under 500 |
| Readings 0 to 16, 18 to 29, 31 to 39 | valid |
| Reading 17 | error from the plugin, prefixed `readings[17]:` |
| Reading 30 | `readings[30]: type: unknown sensor type: humidity.` |
| Answer | `400 {"type":"Validation Error","message":"readings[17]: … readings[30]: …"}`. 0 stored |

### Step 2: store and look up the limits at the same time

```rust
let keys: Vec<(&str, &str)> = readings.iter().map(|r| (r.metric.as_str(), r.room_id.as_str())).collect();
let (inserted, resolved) = tokio::join!(
    self.readings.insert(&readings),
    self.thresholds.resolve(building_id, &keys),
);
inserted?;

let resolved = resolved.unwrap_or_else(|error| {
    log::error!("threshold evaluation failed: {error}");
    Vec::new()
});
```

`tokio::join!` runs the insert and the limit lookup **at the same time**. The insert is one statement with `on conflict do nothing`. The two outcomes are treated differently:

- The insert fails (`inserted?`): the whole call fails with `500`. Nothing is published and no alert goes out.
- The limit lookup fails: it is logged (`threshold evaluation failed`) and treated as "no limits". The readings are stored and published, but there are **no alerts** for that batch.

### Step 3: breaches

```rust
for (reading, bounds) in readings.iter().zip(resolved) {
    self.raise_breach(reading, bounds).await;
}
```

For each reading, `resolved` holds its limits (or nothing). `resolve` picks the **room's** bounds when it has any, else the **building's**. Room bounds replace building bounds; they are not merged.

```rust
pub fn breaches(spec: &[BoundSpec], bounds: &Bounds, payload: &Map<String, Value>) -> Vec<Breach> {
    let mut found: Vec<Breach> = Vec::new();
    for bound in spec {
        if found.iter().any(|seen| seen.bound.field == bound.field) {
            continue;
        }
        let threshold = bounds.get(bound.key).and_then(Value::as_f64);
        let value = payload.get(bound.field).and_then(Value::as_f64);
        let (Some(threshold), Some(value)) = (threshold, value) else { continue; };
        let breached = match bound.direction {
            BoundDirection::Above => value > threshold,
            BoundDirection::Below => value < threshold,
        };
        if breached {
            found.push(Breach { bound: *bound, value, threshold });
        }
    }
    found
}
```

For each limit the plugin declares, in order:

- This `field` already breached? **Skip.** One alert per field, and the first listed limit wins.
- The limit value or the payload value is missing? Skip.
- `Above`: breach when value is greater than the limit. `Below`: breach when it is smaller.

A limit compares **its own** payload field, not the reading's main value. That is why air quality can limit `co2` and `indoor_aqi` separately.

**Example.** Building `b1` has `{"maxTemp":28}`. Room `r2` has `{"maxTemp":24,"minTemp":16}`.

| Reading | Bounds used | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `r1`, 25 °C | building: max 28 | none |
| `r2`, 25 °C | room: max 24, min 16 | `high`, threshold 24 |
| `r2`, 14 °C | room | `low`, threshold 16 |
| `r1`, air quality `co2` 1200, `indoor_aqi` 160, building has `maxCo2` 1000, `maxAqi` 150 | building | **2** alerts: one on `co2`, one on `indoor_aqi` |

```rust
// A failed lookup names the alert by its ids rather than dropping it.
let names = self.buildings.names_of(&reading.building_id).await.unwrap_or_else(|error| {
    log::error!("building name lookup failed: {error}");
    None
});
let building_name = names.as_ref().map_or(&reading.building_id, |n| &n.name);
let room_name = names.as_ref().and_then(|n| n.rooms.get(&reading.room_id)).unwrap_or(&reading.room_id);
for breach in found {
    let alert = AlertPayload { metric, field, building_id, room_id, building_name, room_name,
        value: breach.value, label: breach.bound.label, unit, direction, threshold: breach.threshold, ts_ms: self.clock.now_ms() };
    self.alerts.publish_breach(&alert).await;
}
```

Each breach becomes **its own** alert, with the building and room names from the registration (cached 5 min). A failed name lookup, or an unregistered room, falls back to the ids: an alert with an ugly name beats no alert. The alert is queued to Kafka and not awaited.

### Step 4: the live tick

```rust
let now_ms = self.clock.now_ms();
let events: Vec<TelemetryEvent> = readings.iter().map(|reading| TelemetryEvent::from_reading(reading, now_ms)).collect();
self.fanout.publish_telemetry(&events).await;
Ok(events.len())
```

All the readings become **one** tick on `telemetry:raw`. The answer is `202 {"accepted":true,"readings":40}`.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant G as Device or simulator
        participant V as ingest_auth.rs<br/>verify_signature
        participant H as controllers::ingest
        participant I as kernel::Ingest::accept
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        participant K as Kafka
        participant R as Redis
        G->>V: POST /telemetry/ingest, x-signature, body
        V->>V: read body, at most 1 MiB
        V->>V: peek buildingId, DeviceKeys::accepts
        V->>H: same bytes
        H->>H: readings must be an array
        H->>I: accept(buildingId, readings)
        I->>I: 500 or fewer? building non-empty? list non-empty?
        loop each reading
            I->>I: reading_from: type, plugin, buildingId, plugin.validate
        end
        par
            I->>PG: readings.insert, one statement, on conflict do nothing
        and
            I->>PG: thresholds.resolve, through the 60 s cache
        end
        loop each reading with bounds
            I->>I: raise_breach: breaches(plugin.bounds, bounds, payload)
            I->>K: publish_breach, queued, not awaited
        end
        I->>R: fanout.publish_telemetry, one tick on telemetry:raw
        H-->>G: 202 {"accepted":true,"readings":40}

### What can fail

| Failure | Result |
|---|---|
| Insert fails | `500`. Nothing published, no alert. `telemetry_ingest_persist_failures_total` + 1 |
| Bounds lookup fails | Logged as `threshold evaluation failed`. Readings stored and published, **no alerts** for that batch |
| Kafka refuses an alert | Logged, counted `telemetry_alerts_published_total{outcome="error"}`. The batch is still `202` |
| Redis publish fails | Logged, counted. The batch is still `202` |
| Same batch sent twice | The second insert stores nothing (unique key `building_id, metric, room_id, ts`). Alerts and the tick **are** sent again |

The tick shape and its path to the browser: [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd).

---

## Wi-Fi ticks: `kernel/observations.rs`

`ap-collector` polls the routers about every 10 seconds and sends one snapshot per building.

```rust
pub async fn accept(&self, body: &[u8]) -> Result<(), DomainError> {
    let tick: WifiObservations = serde_json::from_slice(body)
        .map_err(|error| DomainError::Validation(format!("wifi observations: {error}.")))?;
    let problems = tick.problems();
    if !problems.is_empty() {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation(problems.join(" ")));
    }
    let sensors = self.sensors.by_building(&tick.building_id).await?;
    let readings = room_readings(&tick, &sensors);
    if !readings.is_empty() {
        self.ingest.accept(&tick.building_id, &readings).await?;
    }
    Ok(self.events.publish_observations(&tick.building_id, body).await?)
}
```

1. Parse the tick. **Unknown fields are refused**: the bytes travel on to Kafka as received, and a stray `mac` field would travel with them.
2. `problems()` (from the shared crate) checks the tick against the contract. Any problem: `400`, nothing published.
3. Load the building's sensors and turn the tick into **counts per room**.
4. Store those counts through the normal `Ingest` path.
5. Publish the **received bytes** (never a re-serialised copy) to `wifi-observations`, keyed by the building.

### Counting phones per room

```rust
fn room_readings(tick: &WifiObservations, sensors: &[Sensor]) -> Vec<Value> {
    let rooms: HashMap<&str, &str> = sensors.iter()
        .filter(|sensor| sensor.sensor_type == ROUTER)
        .filter_map(|sensor| Some((sensor.sensor_id.as_str(), sensor.room_id.as_deref()?)))
        .collect();

    let mut loudest: HashMap<&str, (i32, &str)> = HashMap::new();
    for phone in &tick.connected {
        let Some(&room) = rooms.get(phone.router.as_str()) else { continue; };
        let heard = (phone.signal, room);
        loudest.entry(phone.token.as_str())
            .and_modify(|best| *best = (*best).max(heard))
            .or_insert(heard);
    }

    let mut counts: BTreeMap<&str, u64> = tick.answered.iter()
        .filter_map(|router| Some((*rooms.get(router.as_str())?, 0)))
        .collect();
    for (_, room) in loudest.into_values() {
        *counts.entry(room).or_default() += 1;
    }
    // … one totalDeviceCount reading per room; ratioDeviceCount too when devicesPerPerson is set
}
```

1. Map each **router** that sits in a room: router id to room id. Outdoor routers and other sensors are left out.
2. For each connected phone on a mapped router, keep its **loudest** hearing: the largest `(signal, room)` pair. On equal signal the room id that sorts last wins.
3. Start every room whose router **answered** at 0.
4. Add 1 to the room of each phone's loudest hearing. A phone counts **once**.
5. One `totalDeviceCount` reading per room, stamped with the tick time.
6. When the tick has `devicesPerPerson`, also `ratioDeviceCount` = count ÷ factor, **rounded up**. The source says why: rounding down 0.4 of a person would report an occupied room as empty.

**Example.** Routers `r1` in `lobby`, `r2` in `hall`. Both answered. Factor 2.5.

| Phone | Heard by | Counts in |
|---|---|---|
| A | `r1` −70, `r2` −60 | `hall` (louder) |
| B | `r1` −55 | `lobby` |
| C | `r1` −60 | `lobby` |
| D | `out` (outdoor router) | nowhere |

Readings: `totalDeviceCount` lobby 2, hall 1. `ratioDeviceCount` lobby ⌈2 ÷ 2.5⌉ = 1, hall ⌈1 ÷ 2.5⌉ = 1.

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Unknown fields refused | The bytes go to Kafka as received. A stray `mac` would travel with them |
| An answered empty room is 0. An unanswered room sends nothing | A failed router did not measure its room. A 0 would look like a fresh empty room |
| Counts stored before the Kafka publish | A count that cannot be stored is `500`, and the tick is not published |
| Kafka refuses | `500`. The collector drops that tick |
| Topic `wifi-observations`, retention 10 min | A tick is stale in seconds, and it is pseudonymous |
| No state between ticks | A phone on a room boundary can switch rooms each tick |

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant A as ap-collector
        participant V as verify_signature
        participant O as kernel::Observations::accept
        participant S as SensorStore
        participant I as Ingest::accept
        participant K as Kafka
        A->>V: POST /telemetry/observations, signed body
        V->>O: same bytes
        O->>O: from_slice into WifiObservations, unknown fields refused
        O->>O: tick.problems(): token, signal, limits
        O->>S: by_building(buildingId)
        O->>O: room_readings(tick, sensors)
        O->>I: accept(buildingId, counts), same path as a batch
        O->>K: publish_observations: the received bytes, key buildingId
        O-->>A: 202 {"accepted":true}

### Room counts back from `digital-twin`

`digital-twin` places each phone more precisely (see [Digital Twin](digital-twin.qd)) and sends room counts back. `telemetry` stores them as a **separate** metric.

```rust
let readings: Vec<Value> = occupancy.rooms.iter().map(|(room, count)| json!({
    "type": ESTIMATED_OCCUPANCY,
    "roomId": room,
    "timestamp": occupancy.tick,
    ESTIMATED_OCCUPANCY: count,
})).collect();
let mut stored = 0;
for batch in readings.chunks(MAX_BATCH_READINGS) {
    stored += self.ingest.accept(&occupancy.building_id, batch).await?;
}
```

Each room becomes an `estimatedOccupancy` reading and goes through the same `Ingest`, in chunks of 500. It is **never** added to `totalDeviceCount`: the two place a phone differently.

- Group `estimated-occupancy`, starts at the earliest offset, commits by hand. After an outage the missed counts are read from the topic.
- An unreadable or refused message is dropped and committed. A store failure is not committed: the consumer reconnects after 5 s and reads it again.
- `estimatedOccupancy` has no bounds and never alerts.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant K as Kafka estimated-occupancy
        participant C as occupancy_consumer.rs
        participant O as kernel::Occupancy
        participant I as Ingest::accept
        K->>C: EstimatedOccupancy for one building
        C->>O: rooms to estimatedOccupancy readings
        loop each chunk of 500
            O->>I: accept(buildingId, chunk)
        end
        C->>K: commit the offset

---

## Building registration: `kernel/registration.rs`

A building must exist here before its sensors can be set up. Two entries lead to one use case, `Registration::register`.

```rust
pub async fn register(&self, building_id: &str, payload: &Value) -> Result<(), DomainError> {
    let request = RegistrationRequest::deserialize(payload)
        .map_err(|error| DomainError::Validation(format!("registration payload: {error}.")))?;

    if request.name.trim().is_empty() {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation("name: must be a non-empty string.".to_owned()));
    }
    // Postgres refuses NUL in text, and a store error is retried, so it would block the topic.
    if std::iter::once(&request.name)
        .chain(request.rooms.iter().flat_map(|room| [&room.id, &room.name]))
        .any(|text| text.contains('\0'))
    {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation("name, rooms: must not contain a NUL character.".to_owned()));
    }

    self.buildings.upsert(&RegisteredBuilding { id: building_id.to_owned(), name: request.name,
        rooms: request.rooms.into_iter().map(|room| Room { id: room.id, name: room.name }).collect() }).await?;

    if let Some(max_temperature) = request.max_temperature {
        let mut patch = Bounds::new();
        patch.insert("maxTemp".to_owned(), max_temperature.into());
        self.thresholds.upsert(building_id, None, "temperature", &patch).await?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

1. Parse the request. Bad shape: `400`-class error.
2. The name must not be blank.
3. **No NUL character** in any name or id. PostgreSQL refuses a NUL in text, a store error would be retried for ever, and that would block the whole topic. So anything the database would refuse is checked first.
4. Save the building and its rooms (an upsert, so registering twice gives the same rows).
5. If `maxTemperature` came with it, save it as the building's `maxTemp`. (Twin does not send it over Kafka today, but the field stays: it is optional, not dead.)

### The Kafka path

```rust
pub async fn register_from_event(&self, building_id: &str, payload: &Value) -> anyhow::Result<bool> {
    // A store outage is not the request's fault: answering `failed` would delete the twin.
    let outcome = match self.register(building_id, payload).await {
        Err(DomainError::Internal(error)) => return Err(error),
        outcome => outcome.map_err(|error| error.to_string()),
    };
    let registered = outcome.is_ok();
    self.events.publish_completed(building_id, outcome).await?;
    Ok(registered)
}
```

- An **invalid** request is answered `failed` with the reason. `digital-twin` then deletes the building.
- A **store error** (`Internal`) is returned as an error and is **not answered**. The consumer does not commit, reconnects after 5 s and reads the message again. A database outage is not the building's fault, and answering `failed` would delete a good building.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant K as Kafka
        participant C as kafka_consumer.rs
        participant G as Registration
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        Tw->>K: building-registration-requested {buildingId, name, rooms}
        K->>C: message, group building-registrations
        C->>C: handle: JSON? has buildingId?
        C->>G: register_from_event(buildingId, payload)
        G->>G: register: parse, name not blank, no NUL character
        G->>PG: upsert building and rooms
        G->>K: building-registration-completed, ready or failed
        C->>K: commit the offset
        Tw->>G: PUT /thresholds/buildings/b1 {name, rooms, maxTemperature}
        G->>PG: same upsert, plus building maxTemp when given

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        M["request message"] --> J{"JSON with buildingId?"}
        J -- no --> D["log, commit, no answer"]
        J -- yes --> R{"register"}
        R -- "valid, stored" --> Ready["answer ready, commit"]
        R -- "invalid: no name, NUL, bad maxTemperature" --> Failed["answer failed with the reason, commit"]
        R -- "store error" --> Retry["no answer, no commit<br/>reconnect after 5 s, read again"]
        Ready --> P{"answer published?"}
        Failed --> P
        P -- no --> Retry

- A room without an id is dropped. A room without a name takes its id.
- The second entry is the HTTP `PUT /thresholds/buildings/{id}` that `digital-twin` calls after an edit. It is the same `register`.

---

## Limits

| Route | Writes | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `PATCH …/buildings/{id}` | Building bounds of one metric | Merged into what is stored (`bounds || patch`). A key not sent keeps its value |
| `PATCH …/buildings/{id}/rooms` | `{roomId: bounds}` for many rooms | Every entry checked before any is written |
| `PATCH …/rooms/{roomId}` | One room | |

Each patch is checked by `Thresholds::checked`: every key must be a bound of that metric's plugin, and every value a number. `{"maxTmp": 28}` on `temperature` is `400 maxTmp: not a bound of temperature.` Because the check asks the **plugin** which keys exist, a new metric needs no change here.

`CachedThresholds` sits in front of the store:

- `resolve` answers from a cache of up to 10 000 entries, each at most 60 s old. One tick asks PostgreSQL once, only for the rooms not cached.
- Any write for building `b1` and metric `temperature` drops **every** cached room of `b1` / `temperature`, because a building row is the fallback for all rooms.
- Another copy of the service sees the change within 60 s.

---

## Sensors batch

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as client
        participant H as controllers::apply_sensors
        participant S as kernel::Sensors::apply
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        C->>H: POST /sensors/buildings/b1 {create, update, delete}
        H->>H: edit check
        H->>S: apply(b1, body)
        S->>S: arrays? 200 items or fewer? refs and ids present?
        S->>PG: rooms of b1 from the registration, sensors of b1
        loop each create, update, delete
            S->>S: check, collect ItemError
        end
        S->>PG: one transaction
        H-->>C: 200 {"created":[{"ref":"new-1","sensorId":"6f1c…"}]}

A batch of changes follows the same idea as ingest: **check everything, then write everything, or nothing**.

| Item | Checks |
|---|---|
| create | `ref` unique in the batch. `name` not blank. `sensorType` a device kind from `/devices`. `roomId` null or a room of the building |
| update | `sensorId` a sensor of this building, once per batch. `name` not blank when given. `roomId` as above when given |
| delete | `sensorId` a sensor of this building, once per batch |

- More than 200 items, or a body that is not an object: `400`.
- Building not registered: `404`.
- Any item bad: `422 {"errors":[{"ref","field","message"}]}`, nothing written. The `ref` lets the screen put each message next to the right row.
- The server makes the id (UUID v4). A `sensorId` sent on create is ignored.
- `roomId` null means **outdoors**.
- Positions are not stored here. They belong to `digital-twin`, under the same `sensorId`.

---

## Simulation

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as client
        participant Si as kernel::Simulation::start
        participant S1 as sensor-simulator
        participant S2 as aq-simulator
        participant S3 as ap-simulator
        C->>Si: PUT /simulation/buildings/b1
        Si->>Si: any simulator configured? building registered?
        Si->>Si: plan: each sensor to the simulator claiming its kind
        par join_all, 5 s timeout each
            Si->>S1: POST /control/start {buildingId, sensors:[temperature, peopleCount]}
        and
            Si->>S2: POST /control/start {buildingId, sensors:[airQuality]}
        and
            Si->>S3: POST /control/start {buildingId, sensors:[]}
        end
        Si-->>C: 204, or 502 when any failed

`telemetry` does not simulate anything itself. It tells each simulator what to simulate, **at start, and only then**.

- Every simulator is told, an empty list included: empty means stop, so a removed sensor stops producing.
- Outdoor sensors and kinds no simulator claims are left out.
- `Simulation::new` refuses a kind that is not a device, or one claimed twice (that would double the readings).
- All calls run at the same time with a 5 s timeout. All are tried, and then any failure is `502`.
- No simulator configured (Kubernetes): start and stop answer `404`.
- Status: `running` when any simulator says so. One that does not answer counts as not running.
- Sensors edited during a run are simulated from the next start.

The body never carries coordinates: positions are `digital-twin`'s. Body shape: [Telemetry Schema, fixture-only shapes](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd).

---

## Device commands

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as client
        participant H as controllers::execute_action
        participant A as kernel::Actions::execute
        participant D as HttpDispatch
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        participant Dev as Device
        C->>H: POST /executeAction {actionData:{metric, buildingId, sensorId, action, arguments}}
        H->>H: fields non-empty, edit check
        H->>A: execute(command)
        A->>A: plugin knows the action? arguments match its parameters?
        A->>D: dispatch(command)
        D->>PG: driver, endpoint of (buildingId, sensorId)
        D->>D: binding = bindings.json[driver][action]
        D->>D: rename arguments, check URL is http or https
        D->>Dev: POST endpoint + path, 5 s timeout
        Dev-->>D: 2xx
        H-->>C: 200 {"accepted":true,"action":"setTarget"}

The kernel checks the command against the **plugin's** action list (the same `FieldSpec` checks as for readings). The adapter then turns it into a real call for that kind of device, using `bindings.json`.

**Example.** `setTarget` with `{"target": 21}` on a sensor with driver `tp-simulator` and endpoint `http://sensor-simulator:3000`. `bindings.json` maps `target` to `value`, so the device gets `POST http://sensor-simulator:3000/control/receive` with `{"value": 21}`.

| Case | Answer |
|---|---|
| Unknown metric or action | `404` |
| Missing or wrong argument | `400` |
| Sensor has no driver or endpoint, or no binding | `404` `no binding for sensor …` |
| Endpoint not http(s) | `404`, logged |
| Device answers non-2xx, or is unreachable | `502` |

The sensor is found by `(buildingId, sensorId)`, **never by room**, so an outdoor or moved sensor can still be reached. The endpoint must be `http` or `https`: the service posts to it, so anything else would let a stored value point it somewhere strange. More: [Sensor Actions](../design/sensor-actions.qd).

---

## Reads

| Route | Answer |
|---|---|
| `latest` | One reading. `roomId` missing is `400`. No data is `404` |
| `entireBuilding` | `{"data": [reading per room]}` |
| `dashboard` | `{"data": [{"timestamp","value"}]}` |
| `connected-devices` | `{buildingId, totalDeviceCount, timestamp}`. Never reported is `404` |

A reading is answered **flat**: `building`, `roomId`, `timestamp`, `value`, plus every plugin field at the top level. The same names as the live tick, so a page that loads history and then live data handles one shape.

`dashboard` query:

| `timeRange` | Window | Bucket |
|---|---|---|
| missing or `1D` | last 24 h | 1 hour |
| `1W` | last 7 days | 1 day |
| `1M` | last 30 days | 1 day |
| `custom` | `start` to `end` (end defaults to now). No `start` is `400` | 1 day |

`aggMode`: `sum`, `min`, `max`, anything else `avg`.

**Example, connected devices.** At 10:00:00 `lobby` reports 12, `hall` 5. `office` last reported at 09:40 (its router was removed). Answer: `{"totalDeviceCount": 17, "timestamp": <10:00:00>}`. `office` is left out: only rooms at the newest report count. The sum is done **here**, never by a client, and only a count adds up: never route an average through it.

---

## What it stores

| Table | Holds | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `readings` | `building_id, room_id, metric, ts, value, payload` | Hypertable. Unique on `building_id, metric, room_id, ts`. Compressed after 7 days, dropped after 14 |
| `readings_hourly` | Hourly summaries | Continuous aggregate, refreshed every hour over the last 3 days |
| `thresholds` | `building_id, room_id, metric, bounds` | `room_id` null = the building row |
| `sensors` | `sensor_id` (key), `building_id, room_id, name, sensor_type, driver, endpoint` | `room_id` null = outdoors |
| `buildings`, `building_rooms` | Registration: names and rooms. `device_key_epoch` | |

Kafka topics it creates: `building-registration-requested`, `building-registration-completed`, `alerts`, `wifi-observations` (10 min).

The unique key `building_id, metric, room_id, ts` is what makes a repeated batch harmless to the archive: the second insert simply stores nothing.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | required | PostgreSQL with TimescaleDB |
| `TELEMETRY_DEVICE_MASTER_KEY` | required, 32 bytes or more | Derives every device key |
| `TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET` | unset | A shared key that signs for any building. Local and simulators only |
| `REDIS_URL` | `redis://redis:6379` | Tick fan-out |
| `KAFKA_BROKERS` | `kafka:9092` | Registration, alerts, Wi-Fi, room counts |
| `DIGITAL_TWIN_URL` | `http://digital-twin:3000` | Ownership lookup |
| `SIMULATORS` | unset | JSON `{name: {url, kinds}}`. Compose gives `temperature`, `peopleCount` to `sensor-simulator`, `airQuality` to `aq-simulator`, `router` to `ap-simulator` |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port |

---

## Metrics

| Name | What it counts |
|---|---|
| `telemetry_ingest_total{metric, outcome}` | Readings `accepted`, batches `invalid`, `unsigned`, `bad_signature` |
| `telemetry_ingest_persist_failures_total`, `…_persist_duration_seconds` | Insert failures and time |
| `telemetry_threshold_breaches_total{metric, direction}` | Breaches found |
| `telemetry_alerts_published_total{topic, outcome}` | Kafka answers to alerts |
| `telemetry_published_total{outcome}` | Redis publishes |
| `telemetry_registration_total{outcome}`, `…_duration_seconds` | `ready`, `failed`, `unacknowledged` |
| `telemetry_occupancy_ticks_total` | Room-count messages |
| `telemetry_query_duration_seconds{query}` | Read times |
| `telemetry_authz_denials_total{action}` | `read`, `edit` denials |
| `telemetry_http_requests_total`, `…_error_requests_total`, `…_duration_seconds` | Every route |
| `telemetry_db_pool_connections` | Pool use |

---

## Tests

```bash
just test telemetry               # unit, kernel against fakes, no database
just test telemetry-integration   # tests/*.rs against throwaway TimescaleDB, Redis, Kafka
```

| Where | What it checks |
|---|---|
| `src/kernel/*.rs` | Every rule above against in-memory fakes: all-or-nothing batch, the 500 cap, every breach its own alert, a failed name lookup still alerts by id, Wi-Fi counting cases, sensors batch errors, simulation plans, actions |
| `src/adapters/ingest_auth.rs` | Golden HMAC vectors shared with Go. The collector request fixture. A key per building. Rotation revokes. The shared key only when set. Short keys refused |
| `src/adapters/driven/*.rs` | Threshold cache: one query per tick, invalidation per building and metric, capacity. Twin lookup cache and timeout. Dispatch renames. Simulator bodies match the fixture |
| `tests/api.rs` | HTTP: `/contracts` equals `fixtures/metric-contract.json`. Every `ingest-batch.json` case and rejection |
| `tests/persistence.rs` | The PostgreSQL adapters |
| `tests/fanout.rs` | A tick reaches `telemetry:raw` |
| `tests/alerts.rs` | A breach reaches `alerts` keyed by building and room, even before anyone subscribes |
| `tests/registration.rs` | Ready answer. A repeat converges. Failed with reason. A bad message does not stop the consumer. A request sent before start is answered. An unpublished answer is read again |
| `tests/cedar_conformance.rs` | Cedar decisions match the shared golden cases |
| `tests/architecture.rs` | The layering rules in the table above |

---

## Gaps

- **One alert per breaching reading.** A room that stays at 31 °C sends an alert with every reading. `notification` mutes repeats for 5 minutes.
- **No "back within limits" message.**
- **A repeated batch alerts again.** The insert drops duplicates, but breaches and the tick are sent a second time.
- **Ingest replay.** A captured, signed batch stays valid. Only the collector `GET` has a time limit.
